e file is byte-identical afterwards. Any mutation that survives, or that is caught only by a different test than the one that claims it, is a finding.\n\nI will also check the boundary semantics the conditions assert rather than assume them: that the named actor is independent of the record signer, that anchor and revocation position boundaries are inclusive at their own records within a shared timestamp, and that NotAfter is inclusive. Inclusive-versus-exclusive at a shared timestamp is where an identity lookup silently returns a revoked or expired actor, and it is the kind of off-by-one no gate catches.\n\nAND I WILL CONFIRM PRODUCTION IS ACTUALLY UNCHANGED rather than take the claim. A diff of host/identity non-test files between the merge-base and 46c7665b either is empty or it is not, and if it is not then the review is a different review from the one this request describes.\n\nArchitecture, Security and Simplification conclusions per AGENTS.md, with docs/reference/architecture.md treatment named explicitly: whether the host/identity contract changes and, if it does, whether the page moves in the same head. Security covers the identity, anchor, expiry and revocation authority boundaries and their failure modes, with particular attention to whether any of them fails open. Simplification names removable test fluff only where removing it would not weaken a condition.\n\nI will file the verdict with gs review against the exact artifact and head, so it rests on de8950ef rather than on my own prose. This review touches none of my publication, freshness or decision-records lanes and publishes no artifact, so it does not disturb the settled freshness-then-guard-then-publication order."}